Instead of creating a complete sorted array, this uses two stacks. One stack produces values from smallest to largest, and the other produces values from largest to smallest. Together they behave like the two ends of a sorted array.

For example, with `2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8` and target `12`, the values begin at `2` and `8`. Their sum is too small, so the forward iterator moves to `3`, then `4`, where the target pair is found. The tree itself stays unchanged.

```java
import java.util.Stack;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    private static class BSTIterator {
        private final Stack<TreeNode> stack = new Stack<>();
        private final boolean forward;

        BSTIterator(TreeNode root, boolean forward) {
            this.forward = forward;
            pushPath(root);
        }

        private void pushPath(TreeNode node) {
            while (node != null) {
                stack.push(node);
                node = forward ? node.left : node.right;
            }
        }

        boolean hasNext() {
            return !stack.isEmpty();
        }

        int next() {
            TreeNode node = stack.pop();
            if (forward) {
                pushPath(node.right);
            } else {
                pushPath(node.left);
            }
            return node.data;
        }
    }

    public boolean twoSumBST(TreeNode root, int k) {
        if (root == null) {
            return false;
        }

        BSTIterator low = new BSTIterator(root, true);
        BSTIterator high = new BSTIterator(root, false);

        int leftValue = low.next();
        int rightValue = high.next();

        while (leftValue < rightValue) {
            long sum = (long) leftValue + rightValue;

            if (sum == k) {
                return true;
            }
            if (sum < k) {
                if (!low.hasNext()) {
                    break;
                }
                leftValue = low.next();
            } else {
                if (!high.hasNext()) {
                    break;
                }
                rightValue = high.next();
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, as each iterator processes each needed node only once.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the two stacks, where `h` is the tree height.

The BST property gives a sorted list if the tree is visited in inorder. This code stores that list, then uses two pointers at the beginning and end. A small sum moves the left pointer forward, while a large sum moves the right pointer backward.

For values `[2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]` and target `9`, the first check is `2 + 7`, so the answer is immediately `true`. The input tree is not changed, but the vector needs extra memory.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution{
public:
    void inorder(TreeNode* node, vector<int>& values) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return;
        }

        inorder(node->left, values);
        values.push_back(node->data);
        inorder(node->right, values);
    }

    bool twoSumBST(TreeNode* root, int k){
        vector<int> values;
        inorder(root, values);

        int left = 0;
        int right = static_cast<int>(values.size()) - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            long long sum = static_cast<long long>(values[left]) + values[right];

            if (sum == k) {
                return true;
            }
            if (sum < k) {
                ++left;
            } else {
                --right;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` for the traversal and pointer scan.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the sorted vector, plus `O(h)` recursion space.

Here, each node is treated as one number of the pair. The other number must be `k - node.data`, so a normal BST search checks whether that value exists. The search skips the current node, which prevents using one node twice.

This uses the BST ordering directly, but it may repeat searches many times. That makes it less attractive than a hash set for a large tree.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public boolean twoSumBST(TreeNode root, int k) {
        return checkNodes(root, root, k);
    }

    private boolean checkNodes(TreeNode root, TreeNode current, int k) {
        if (current == null) {
            return false;
        }

        long needed = (long) k - current.data;
        if (findValue(root, current, needed)) {
            return true;
        }

        return checkNodes(root, current.left, k)
                || checkNodes(root, current.right, k);
    }

    private boolean findValue(TreeNode node, TreeNode ignored, long target) {
        if (node == null) {
            return false;
        }

        if (node != ignored && node.data == target) {
            return true;
        }

        if (target < node.data) {
            return findValue(node.left, ignored, target);
        }
        return findValue(node.right, ignored, target);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(nh)`, because a BST search can take `O(h)` for each of `n` nodes.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursive calls. Here `h` is the tree height.

An empty tree or a single-node tree correctly returns `false`.

This keeps the implementation very direct. First, inorder traversal puts all node values into a slice. Then every pair is checked with two indexes. It does not rely on the BST ordering after the traversal, so it is easy to compare with the basic two sum idea, but it is slower than the other versions.

The indexes always satisfy `first < second`, so the same node cannot be selected twice.

```go
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * type TreeNode struct {
 *     Data int
 *     Left *TreeNode
 *     Right *TreeNode
 * }
 */

func twoSumBST(root *TreeNode, k int) bool {
    values := make([]int, 0)

    var inorder func(*TreeNode)
    inorder = func(node *TreeNode) {
        if node == nil {
            return
        }
        inorder(node.Left)
        values = append(values, node.Data)
        inorder(node.Right)
    }

    inorder(root)

    for first := 0; first < len(values); first++ {
        for second := first + 1; second < len(values); second++ {
            if int64(values[first])+int64(values[second]) == int64(k) {
                return true
            }
        }
    }

    return false
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` for checking all pairs, with `O(n)` for traversal included.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the slice and `O(h)` for recursion.

A tree with no nodes simply creates an empty slice and returns `false`.

## Intuition
First, perform inorder traversal of the BST and store all node values in a vector.
Inorder traversal of a BST gives values in sorted order.
Then use a hash map to check whether k - current_value has already appeared.
If found, two nodes exist whose sum is k.

## Approach
Inorder Traversal: Traverse the BST using inorder and store all node values in a vector. Since it's a BST, the values will be in sorted order.
Hash Map: For every value x, calculate k - x.
Check if k - x already exists in the hash map.
If yes → return true.
Otherwise, store x in the map.
If no pair is found, return false.

